## std/fractio.pengu — the fraction: methods that only make sense on
## floating-point values, over the `Real` concept.
##
## `fractio` dota de métodos a `float`, `f32`, `f64` y `double`. La
## clasificación IEEE 754, la aproximación y las utilidades puras viven en un
## único bloque `enchanting shard T where T: Real:`; los transcendentales, que
## llaman a `libm`, viven en dos bloques concretos (`enchanting float:` y
## `enchanting f64:`) porque C expone símbolos distintos por ancho (`sqrtf`
## frente a `sqrt`). El compilador monomorfiza cada método por tipo concreto
## (`float_is_nan`, `f64_sqrt`, …), así que no hay vtables, boxing ni coste en
## runtime más allá de la llamada a `libm`.
##
## ## Alcance
## `fractio` cubre **solo** lo que tiene sentido en flotantes: clasificación
## IEEE 754 (`is_nan`, `is_finite`, `is_infinite`, `is_positive_infinity`,
## `is_negative_infinity`), comparación con tolerancia (`approx_eq`),
## interpolación (`lerp`), utilidades (`reciprocal`, `copysign`, `degrees`,
## `radians`) y transcendentales (`sqrt`, `cbrt`, `sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `asin`,
## `acos`, `atan`, `atan2`, `exp`, `ln`, `log2`, `log10`, `pow`, `hypot`).
## Los métodos universales (`abs`, `signum`, `clamp`, `round`, `pow_int`, …)
## viven en `std.numerus`; los exclusivos de enteros, en `std.integrum`.
## `Real` implica `Num`, así que un programa que importe ambos tiene todo.
##
## ## Delegación
## Este módulo es la **fuente de verdad** de la matemática de punto flotante:
## los transcendentales de `std.arithmancy` (`sqrt`, `pow`, `sin`, `cos`,
## `tan`, `asin`, `acos`, `atan`, `atan2`, `exp`, `log`, `log10`, `log2`,
## `hypot`, `lerp`, `deg_to_rad`, `rad_to_deg`) son ahora wrappers de una línea
## sobre estos métodos. `arithmancy.approx_eq` **no** delega: usa `<=` y éste
## `<`, así que difieren justo cuando `|a - b| == eps`.
## `fractio` **no** importa `std.numerus`: no necesita ningún método universal
## (las dos magnitudes que calcula, en `approx_eq` y `copysign`, son tres líneas
## cada una) y un módulo hoja de matemáticas gana más quedándose sin
## dependencias cruzadas que ahorrándose esos dos bucles. Un programa que
## importe ambos sigue teniendo `abs`, `clamp`, `pow_int`, … porque `Real`
## implica `Num` (`LANGUAGE.md` §11.3).
##
## ## Memoria
## Ningún método de este módulo asigna memoria del heap. No hay nada que
## liberar con `banish`.
##
## ## Precondiciones
## Ninguna función panickea: el comportamiento en los casos límite es el de
## C99 (`<math.h>`), con NaN e Inf propagados según IEEE 754.
## - `sqrt`, `ln`, `log2`, `log10`: dominio `self >= 0` (`> 0` para los
##   logaritmos). Fuera del dominio devuelven NaN (`ln(0)` devuelve -Inf).
## - `asin`, `acos`: dominio `[-1, 1]`; fuera devuelven NaN.
## - `reciprocal`: `self != 0`; con `0.0` devuelve ±Inf según el signo del cero.
## - `approx_eq` usa tolerancia **absoluta**. Para magnitudes grandes escala
##   `eps` con `|self|` (`1e20 * 1e-9`), no lo dejes fijo.
##
## ## Nota de implementación
## Los `declare` nombran los símbolos **reales** de `libm` (`sqrtf`, `sqrt`, …):
## `pengu_runtime.h` ya incluye `<math.h>`, así que sus prototipos están
## disponibles y no hace falta una capa `pengu_c_*` por ancho. Los literales
## numéricos de un cuerpo `T: Real` se escriben `(1.0 to T)`: un literal `int`
## no es convertible a `T: Real` (`int` no implementa `Real`), un literal
## flotante sí.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## import std.spark
## import std.fractio
##
## weave main into int:
##     var x as f64 is 4.0
##     var y as f64 is calling x.sqrt
##     calling spark.println with "{y}"          # 2
##     var neg as f64 is -1.0
##     var bad as f64 is calling neg.sqrt
##     if calling bad.is_nan:
##         calling spark.println with "NaN"      # NaN
##     return 0
## ```

include "math.h"

import std.ward

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Module Version (LANGUAGE.md §19.0: every hand-written std module exports one)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Fractio module version.
const FRACTIO_VERSION as string is "2.0.0"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# libm declarations (symbols are the real C99 ones; <math.h> is already
# included by pengu_runtime.h, and the bundle links -lm)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

declare sqrtf with x as f32 into f32
declare sqrt with x as f64 into f64
declare cbrtf with x as f32 into f32
declare cbrt with x as f64 into f64
declare sinf with x as f32 into f32
declare sin with x as f64 into f64
declare cosf with x as f32 into f32
declare cos with x as f64 into f64
declare tanf with x as f32 into f32
declare tan with x as f64 into f64
declare asinf with x as f32 into f32
declare asin with x as f64 into f64
declare acosf with x as f32 into f32
declare acos with x as f64 into f64
declare atanf with x as f32 into f32
declare atan with x as f64 into f64
declare atan2f with y as f32, x as f32 into f32
declare atan2 with y as f64, x as f64 into f64
declare expf with x as f32 into f32
declare exp with x as f64 into f64
declare logf with x as f32 into f32
declare log with x as f64 into f64
declare log2f with x as f32 into f32
declare log2 with x as f64 into f64
declare log10f with x as f32 into f32
declare log10 with x as f64 into f64
declare powf with base as f32, exponent as f32 into f32
declare pow with base as f64, exponent as f64 into f64
declare hypotf with x as f32, y as f32 into f32
declare hypot with x as f64, y as f64 into f64

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pure methods (no libm): one body for all four float types
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

enchanting shard T where T: Real:

    # ─── Clasificación IEEE 754 ──────────────────────────────────────────

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self` es NaN (Not a Number).
    ##
    ## Funciona sin `libm`: `NaN != NaN` es siempre `true` por IEEE 754.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var x as f64 is 1.0
    ## var neg as f64 is -1.0
    ## var bad as f64 is calling neg.sqrt
    ## calling spark.println with "{calling bad.is_nan}"   # true
    ## ```
    weave is_nan into bool:
        return self != self

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self` es +Inf o -Inf.
    ##
    ## Implementación puramente PenguScript: para un valor finito `x`,
    ## `|x| - |x| == 0`; para `±Inf`, `Inf - Inf` es NaN y `NaN != 0` es
    ## `true`. NaN se descarta primero para no confundirse con infinito.
    weave is_infinite into bool:
        if calling self.is_nan:
            return false
        var a as T is self
        if a < donum T:
            set a is -a
        return (a - a) != donum T

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self` es finito (ni NaN ni ±Inf).
    weave is_finite into bool:
        return (not (calling self.is_infinite)) and (not (calling self.is_nan))

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self` es exactamente `+Inf`.
    weave is_positive_infinity into bool:
        return (self > donum T) and (calling self.is_infinite)

    ## Devuelve `true` si `self` es exactamente `-Inf`.
    weave is_negative_infinity into bool:
        return (self < donum T) and (calling self.is_infinite)

    # ─── Aproximación ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ## Devuelve `true` si `|self - other| < eps`.
    ##
    ## ## Semántica
    ## La tolerancia `eps` es **absoluta**, no relativa. Para magnitudes
    ## grandes, escala `eps` con `|self|`: comparar `1e20` con `1e20 + 1`
    ## necesita `eps` proporcional, no `1e-9`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var a as f64 is 0.1 + 0.2
    ## var b as f64 is 0.3
    ## calling spark.println with "{calling a.approx_eq with b, 1e-9}"   # true
    ## ```
    weave approx_eq with other as T, eps as T into bool:
        var d as T is self - other
        if d < donum T:
            set d is -d
        return d < eps

    # ─── Utilidades ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ## Devuelve `1.0 / self`.
    ##
    ## ## Precondiciones
    ## `self != 0.0`. Con `0.0` devuelve `+Inf` (o `-Inf` para `-0.0`), según
    ## IEEE 754; no aborta.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var x as f64 is 2.0
    ## calling spark.println with "{calling x.reciprocal}"   # 0.5
    ## ```
    weave reciprocal into T:
        return (1.0 to T) / self

    ## Interpolación lineal: `self + t * (other - self)`.
    ##
    ## ## Semántica
    ## `t` no se acota: `t == 0` devuelve `self`, `t == 1` devuelve `other`, y
    ## fuera de `[0, 1]` extrapola. Para acotar, aplica
    ## `calling t.clamp with 0.0, 1.0` (de `std.numerus`).
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var a as f64 is 0.0
    ## var b as f64 is 10.0
    ## calling spark.println with "{calling a.lerp with b, 0.5}"   # 5
    ## ```
    weave lerp with other as T, t as T into T:
        return self + (t * (other - self))

    ## Devuelve `|self|` con el signo de `sign_source`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var x as f64 is 3.0
    ## var y as f64 is -1.0
    ## calling spark.println with "{calling x.copysign with y}"   # -3
    ## ```
    weave copysign with sign_source as T into T:
        var magnitude as T is self
        if magnitude < donum T:
            set magnitude is -magnitude
        if sign_source < donum T:
            return -magnitude
        return magnitude

    ## Convierte `self` de radianes a grados: `self * 180 / π`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var pi as f64 is 3.141592653589793
    ## calling spark.println with "{calling pi.degrees}"   # ~180
    ## ```
    weave degrees into T:
        return self * ((180.0 to T) / (3.141592653589793 to T))

    ## Convierte `self` de grados a radianes: `self * π / 180`.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## var d as f64 is 180.0
    ## calling spark.println with "{calling d.radians}"   # ~3.14159
    ## ```
    weave radians into T:
        return self * ((3.141592653589793 to T) / (180.0 to T))

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Transcendentals on `float` (covers `float` and `f32`; C has one `float` type)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

enchanting float:

    ## Raíz cuadrada. Dominio `self >= 0`; un negativo devuelve NaN.
    weave sqrt into float:
        return calling sqrtf with self

    ## Raíz cúbica (definida para cualquier signo).
    weave cbrt into float:
        return calling cbrtf with self

    ## Seno (radianes).
    weave sin into float:
        return calling sinf with self

    ## Coseno (radianes).
    weave cos into float:
        return calling cosf with self

    ## Tangente (radianes).
    weave tan into float:
        return calling tanf with self

    ## Arcoseno. Dominio `[-1, 1]`; fuera devuelve NaN.
    weave asin into float:
        return calling asinf with self

    ## Arcocoseno. Dominio `[-1, 1]`; fuera devuelve NaN.
    weave acos into float:
        return calling acosf with self

    ## Arcotangente.
    weave atan into float:
        return calling atanf with self

    ## Ángulo cuyo seno es `self` y cuyo coseno es `x`, en el cuadrante
    ## correcto (radianes).
    weave atan2 with x as float into float:
        return calling atan2f with self, x

    ## Exponencial natural (`e^self`).
    weave exp into float:
        return calling expf with self

    ## Logaritmo natural. Dominio `self > 0`; `0` devuelve `-Inf` y un negativo
    ## NaN.
    weave ln into float:
        return calling logf with self

    ## Logaritmo en base 2. Dominio `self > 0`.
    weave log2 into float:
        return calling log2f with self

    ## Logaritmo en base 10. Dominio `self > 0`.
    weave log10 into float:
        return calling log10f with self

    ## `self` elevado a `exponent`.
    weave pow with exponent as float into float:
        return calling powf with self, exponent

    ## `sqrt(self² + other²)` sin desbordamiento intermedio.
    weave hypot with other as float into float:
        return calling hypotf with self, other

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Transcendentals on `f64` (covers `f64` and `double`)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

enchanting f64:

    ## Raíz cuadrada. Dominio `self >= 0`; un negativo devuelve NaN.
    weave sqrt into f64:
        return calling sqrt with self

    ## Raíz cúbica (definida para cualquier signo).
    weave cbrt into f64:
        return calling cbrt with self

    ## Seno (radianes).
    weave sin into f64:
        return calling sin with self

    ## Coseno (radianes).
    weave cos into f64:
        return calling cos with self

    ## Tangente (radianes).
    weave tan into f64:
        return calling tan with self

    ## Arcoseno. Dominio `[-1, 1]`; fuera devuelve NaN.
    weave asin into f64:
        return calling asin with self

    ## Arcocoseno. Dominio `[-1, 1]`; fuera devuelve NaN.
    weave acos into f64:
        return calling acos with self

    ## Arcotangente.
    weave atan into f64:
        return calling atan with self

    ## Ángulo cuyo seno es `self` y cuyo coseno es `x`, en el cuadrante
    ## correcto (radianes).
    weave atan2 with x as f64 into f64:
        return calling atan2 with self, x

    ## Exponencial natural (`e^self`).
    weave exp into f64:
        return calling exp with self

    ## Logaritmo natural. Dominio `self > 0`; `0` devuelve `-Inf` y un negativo
    ## NaN.
    weave ln into f64:
        return calling log with self

    ## Logaritmo en base 2. Dominio `self > 0`.
    weave log2 into f64:
        return calling log2 with self

    ## Logaritmo en base 10. Dominio `self > 0`.
    weave log10 into f64:
        return calling log10 with self

    ## `self` elevado a `exponent`.
    weave pow with exponent as f64 into f64:
        return calling pow with self, exponent

    ## `sqrt(self² + other²)` sin desbordamiento intermedio.
    weave hypot with other as f64 into f64:
        return calling hypot with self, other

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Helper de test: falla salvo que `got` sea `true`.
weave _t_true with got as bool into void:
    calling ward.assert_true with got

## Helper de test: falla salvo que `got` sea `false`.
weave _t_false with got as bool into void:
    calling ward.assert_false with got

## Helper de test: igualdad exacta de `f64`.
weave _t_f64 with got as f64, want as f64 into void:
    if got != want:
        calling ward.assert_true with false

## Helper de test: igualdad aproximada de `f64` dentro de `eps` absoluto.
weave _t_close with got as f64, want as f64, eps as f64 into void:
    var d as f64 is got - want
    if d < 0.0:
        set d is -d
    if d > eps:
        calling ward.assert_true with false

## Helper de test: `NaN` de `f64`.
weave _t_nan with got as f64 into void:
    if not (got != got):
        calling ward.assert_true with false

test "is_nan detects NaN from sqrt of a negative":
    var neg as f64 is -1.0
    var bad as f64 is calling neg.sqrt
    calling _t_true with (calling bad.is_nan)

test "is_nan is false for ordinary values":
    var x as f64 is 1.5
    var z as f64 is 0.0
    calling _t_false with (calling x.is_nan)
    calling _t_false with (calling z.is_nan)

test "is_finite is true for ordinary values":
    var x as f64 is 1.5
    var z as f64 is 0.0
    var n as f64 is -3.25
    calling _t_true with (calling x.is_finite)
    calling _t_true with (calling z.is_finite)
    calling _t_true with (calling n.is_finite)

test "is_finite is false for Inf and NaN":
    var zero as f64 is 0.0
    var inf as f64 is calling zero.reciprocal
    var neg as f64 is -1.0
    var bad as f64 is calling neg.sqrt
    calling _t_false with (calling inf.is_finite)
    calling _t_false with (calling bad.is_finite)

test "is_infinite detects positive infinity":
    var zero as f64 is 0.0
    var inf as f64 is calling zero.reciprocal
    calling _t_true with (calling inf.is_infinite)

test "is_infinite detects negative infinity":
    var zero as f64 is 0.0
    var ninf as f64 is (0.0 - 1.0) / zero
    calling _t_true with (calling ninf.is_infinite)

test "is_infinite is false for finite values and NaN":
    var x as f64 is 2.0
    var z as f64 is 0.0
    var neg as f64 is -1.0
    var bad as f64 is calling neg.sqrt
    calling _t_false with (calling x.is_infinite)
    calling _t_false with (calling z.is_infinite)
    calling _t_false with (calling bad.is_infinite)

test "is_positive_infinity distinguishes the sign":
    var zero as f64 is 0.0
    var inf as f64 is calling zero.reciprocal
    var ninf as f64 is (0.0 - 1.0) / zero
    var one as f64 is 1.0
    calling _t_true with (calling inf.is_positive_infinity)
    calling _t_false with (calling ninf.is_positive_infinity)
    calling _t_false with (calling one.is_positive_infinity)

test "is_negative_infinity distinguishes the sign":
    var zero as f64 is 0.0
    var inf as f64 is calling zero.reciprocal
    var ninf as f64 is (0.0 - 1.0) / zero
    var one as f64 is 1.0
    calling _t_true with (calling ninf.is_negative_infinity)
    calling _t_false with (calling inf.is_negative_infinity)
    calling _t_false with (calling one.is_negative_infinity)

test "approx_eq accepts exact and near values":
    var a as f64 is 1.0
    var b as f64 is 1.0
    calling _t_true with (calling a.approx_eq with b, 1e-9)
    var c as f64 is 0.1 + 0.2
    var d as f64 is 0.3
    calling _t_true with (calling c.approx_eq with d, 1e-9)

test "approx_eq rejects values outside eps":
    var a as f64 is 1.0
    var b as f64 is 1.001
    calling _t_false with (calling a.approx_eq with b, 1e-9)
    calling _t_true with (calling a.approx_eq with b, 1e-2)

test "approx_eq works with negatives":
    var a as f64 is -2.5
    var b as f64 is -2.5
    var c as f64 is -2.6
    calling _t_true with (calling a.approx_eq with b, 1e-9)
    calling _t_false with (calling a.approx_eq with c, 1e-9)

test "reciprocal inverts the value":
    var a as f64 is 2.0
    var b as f64 is -1.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling a.reciprocal), 0.5
    calling _t_f64 with (calling b.reciprocal), -1.0

test "reciprocal of zero is infinity":
    var zero as f64 is 0.0
    var inf as f64 is calling zero.reciprocal
    calling _t_true with (calling inf.is_infinite)

test "lerp interpolates at the endpoints and the middle":
    var a as f64 is 0.0
    var b as f64 is 10.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling a.lerp with b, 0.0), 0.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling a.lerp with b, 1.0), 10.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling a.lerp with b, 0.5), 5.0

test "lerp extrapolates outside the unit interval":
    var a as f64 is 0.0
    var b as f64 is 10.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling a.lerp with b, 2.0), 20.0

test "copysign keeps the magnitude and takes the sign":
    var p as f64 is 3.0
    var n as f64 is -3.0
    var neg as f64 is -1.0
    var pos as f64 is 1.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling p.copysign with neg), -3.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling n.copysign with pos), 3.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling p.copysign with pos), 3.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling n.copysign with neg), -3.0

test "degrees turns pi into 180":
    var pi as f64 is 3.141592653589793
    calling _t_close with (calling pi.degrees), 180.0, 1e-9
    var half as f64 is pi / 2.0
    calling _t_close with (calling half.degrees), 90.0, 1e-9

test "radians turns 180 into pi":
    var d as f64 is 180.0
    var pi as f64 is 3.141592653589793
    calling _t_close with (calling d.radians), pi, 1e-12

test "sqrt of a perfect square is exact":
    var a as f64 is 4.0
    var b as f64 is 9.0
    var c as float is 16.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling a.sqrt), 2.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling b.sqrt), 3.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling c.sqrt to f64), 4.0

test "sqrt of a negative is NaN":
    var neg as f64 is -1.0
    var bad as f64 is calling neg.sqrt
    calling _t_nan with bad

test "cbrt handles both signs":
    var a as f64 is 8.0
    var b as f64 is -8.0
    var z as f64 is 0.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling a.cbrt), 2.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling b.cbrt), -2.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling z.cbrt), 0.0

test "sin and cos at the cardinal angles":
    var zero as f64 is 0.0
    var pi as f64 is 3.141592653589793
    var half as f64 is pi / 2.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling zero.sin), 0.0
    calling _t_close with (calling half.sin), 1.0, 1e-12
    calling _t_f64 with (calling zero.cos), 1.0
    calling _t_close with (calling half.cos), 0.0, 1e-12

test "tan at zero and pi/4":
    var zero as f64 is 0.0
    var pi as f64 is 3.141592653589793
    var quarter as f64 is pi / 4.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling zero.tan), 0.0
    calling _t_close with (calling quarter.tan), 1.0, 1e-12

test "asin and acos at the domain edges":
    var one as f64 is 1.0
    var zero as f64 is 0.0
    var pi as f64 is 3.141592653589793
    var half as f64 is pi / 2.0
    calling _t_close with (calling one.asin), half, 1e-12
    calling _t_f64 with (calling zero.asin), 0.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling one.acos), 0.0
    calling _t_close with (calling zero.acos), half, 1e-12

test "asin outside the domain is NaN":
    var two as f64 is 2.0
    var bad as f64 is calling two.asin
    calling _t_nan with bad

test "atan and atan2 in the first quadrant":
    var one as f64 is 1.0
    var pi as f64 is 3.141592653589793
    var quarter as f64 is pi / 4.0
    calling _t_close with (calling one.atan), quarter, 1e-12
    calling _t_close with (calling one.atan2 with one), quarter, 1e-12

test "exp at zero and one":
    var zero as f64 is 0.0
    var one as f64 is 1.0
    var e as f64 is 2.718281828459045
    calling _t_f64 with (calling zero.exp), 1.0
    calling _t_close with (calling one.exp), e, 1e-12

test "ln is the inverse of exp":
    var one as f64 is 1.0
    var e as f64 is 2.718281828459045
    calling _t_f64 with (calling one.ln), 0.0
    calling _t_close with (calling e.ln), 1.0, 1e-12

test "ln of zero is negative infinity":
    var zero as f64 is 0.0
    var ninf as f64 is calling zero.ln
    calling _t_true with (calling ninf.is_negative_infinity)

test "ln of a negative is NaN":
    var neg as f64 is -1.0
    var bad as f64 is calling neg.ln
    calling _t_nan with bad

test "log2 and log10 on the powers of their base":
    var one as f64 is 1.0
    var eight as f64 is 8.0
    var thousand as f64 is 1024.0
    var hundred as f64 is 100.0
    var thousand10 as f64 is 1000.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling one.log2), 0.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling eight.log2), 3.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling thousand.log2), 10.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling hundred.log10), 2.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling thousand10.log10), 3.0

test "pow raises to a real exponent":
    var two as f64 is 2.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling two.pow with 10.0), 1024.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling two.pow with 0.0), 1.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling two.pow with -1.0), 0.5

test "hypot is the Pythagorean length":
    var three as f64 is 3.0
    var four as f64 is 4.0
    var zero as f64 is 0.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling three.hypot with four), 5.0
    calling _t_f64 with (calling zero.hypot with zero), 0.0

test "the transcendental methods are monomorphized per width":
    var wide as f64 is 2.25
    var narrow as float is 2.25
    calling _t_close with (calling wide.sqrt), 1.5, 1e-12
    calling _t_close with ((calling narrow.sqrt to f64)), 1.5, 1e-6

test "float and f32 aliases reach the same methods":
    var a as f32 is 4.0
    var b as double is 4.0
    calling _t_close with ((calling a.sqrt to f64)), 2.0, 1e-6
    calling _t_close with (calling b.sqrt), 2.0, 1e-12

test "fractio and numerus compose when both are imported elsewhere":
    # `fractio` deliberately does not import `std.numerus` (see the module
    # docstring): this test only proves the `Real`->`Num` chain at the type
    # level, using `fractio`'s own methods.
    var x as f64 is 2.25
    var r as f64 is calling x.reciprocal
    calling _t_close with (calling r.reciprocal), x, 1e-12
    calling _t_true with (calling x.is_finite)
